  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nSurface low pressure was analyzed just southeast of KMSP as of \n1 AM CST, with a trailing cold front extending to its south, \njust west of the Mississippi River over far eastern IA/MO. \nAloft, GOES water vapor imagery depicted a strong negative-tilt \nshort wave lifting northeast across MO toward western IL. Strong\nforcing ahead of this wave, combined with moisture advection on\nenhanced 45-50 kt 850-500 mb south-southwesterly flow, \ncontinues to produce an area of rain from the lower Ohio Valley \nnorthwest across the region. This area of rain will continue to \nshift to the north-northeast, with the trailing edge of the more\norganized precipitation along the I-39 corridor eventually \nmoving east of the forecast area this morning. Though drier mid-\nlevel air will spread across the area, lingering low-level \nmoisture will likely continue to support the potential for some \npatchy light rain or drizzle at times through the day. Strong \nsouth winds sporadically gusting 25-30+ mph ahead of the cold \nfront overnight have held temperatures in the 60s overnight, \nthough as winds shift southwest and ease a bit with the cold \nfrontal passage today, temperatures will gradually fall into the\n50s. Lingering cloud cover and breezy southwest winds tonight \nwill keep temperatures in the 45-50 degree range.\n\nThe surface low to our north will only slowly drift northeast \nacross the northern Great Lakes this afternoon into Thursday, as\nit occludes and becomes stacked beneath a slow-moving upper \nlow/trough. This upper trough will be slow to move east over the\nnext couple of days, as a series of smaller-amplitude short \nwaves rotate about. This will maintain the potential for periods\nof scattered showers across the area at times, most notably \nThursday into Thursday night, as a secondary cold front pushes \nsoutheast across the area. Highs in the 50s are expected again \nThursday, with temperatures dropping into the upper 30s/low 40s \nThursday night in breezy north flow behind the cold front.\n\nGuidance is in decent agreement in digging a short wave \nsouthward into the upper trough over the Great Lakes region \nThursday night, and developing a new closed upper-level low over\nlower MI by early Friday. This looks to reinforce low-level \ncold advection across the western Great Lakes region, setting up\nlake-effect showers over Lake Michigan and northwest IN Friday \ninto Friday night, before the upper trough axis shifts \neast/southeast, and eventually lowers inversion heights over \nthe lake. Model ensembles then indicate surface high pressure \nbuilds south across the upper Midwest and Mississippi Valley \nFriday night into Saturday. Decreasing cloud cover and \ndiminishing winds associated with the surface ridge, combined \nwith sub-zero H8 temps may set the stage for freezing conditions\nespecially across the IL portion of our CWA late Friday \nnight/early Saturday morning. Confidence is lower in a freeze \nover northwest IN, downstream of the warmer lake and potentially\nwith some lingering lake-effect clouds. Dry weather is then \nexpected Saturday/Sunday as the surface continues to drift \nsoutheast. Near average temperatures are forecast (highs in the \n50s, lows mainly in the 30s). Ensembles suggest upper ridging \ndevelops into the region early next week, with temperatures \npotentially trending slightly above average again Monday-\nTuesday.\n\nRatzer",
